\li1440\sl-480\slmult0\f53\fs48\expnd0\expndtw-2 DE-IDENTIFICATION\expnd0\expndtw0  \expnd0\expndtw-2 PATTERN\expnd0\expndtw0 \par\pard\sl-200\slmult0\fs20 \par\pard\sl-194\slmult0\fs18 \par\pard\li104\ri121\sl-330\slmult0\f19\fs28\expnd-1\expndtw-3\charscalex99 T\expnd0\expndtw0 his pattern is useful for going b\expnd-1\expndtw-4 e\expnd0\expndtw0 yond limiting (and therefore false)\f19  identifications, usually in the form of a CEq.   Complex equivalence’s are\f19  the language construction of identifica\expnd0\expndtw-2 t\expnd0\expndtw0 ion.  With a slight adjustment of\f19  the language you can use this easily on yourself therapeutically or as a\f19  meditation if you continue to repeat steps 3 through 6.\charscalex100 \par\pard\sl-200\slmult0\fs20 \par\pard\sl-134\slmult0\fs12 \par\pard\li553\fi-450\ri274\sl-330\slmult0\f19\fs28\charscalex99 1.  Elicit the identification in the form of a Complex Equivalence. Listen\f19  for:\charscalex100 \par\pard\li553\sl-340\slmult0\sb6\f60\fs28\charscalex129 •\f37\charscalex100   \expnd4\expndtw21  \f19\expnd0\expndtw-2\charscalex99 the\expnd0\expndtw0  \expnd0\expndtw-2 verb\expnd0\expndtw0  \expnd0\expndtw-2 “to\expnd0\expndtw0  \expnd0\expndtw-2 be”\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 \par\pard\li553\sl-340\slmult0\sb6\f60\fs28\charscalex129 •\f37\charscalex100   \expnd4\expndtw21  \f19\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex99 Or "Means"\charscalex100 \par\pard\sl-200\slmult0\fs20 \par\pard\sl-184\slmult0\fs18 \par\pard\li103\sl-280\slmult0\f19\fs28\expnd0\expndtw-2\charscalex99 2\expnd0\expndtw0 .\charscalex100  \expnd2\expndtw8  \expnd0\expndtw-2\charscalex99 Pace\expnd0\expndtw0  \expnd0\expndtw-2 and\expnd0\expndtw0  \expnd0\expndtw-2 feedback\expnd0\expndtw0  \expnd0\expndtw-2 the\expnd0\expndtw0  \expnd0\expndtw-2 compl\expnd-1\expndtw-3 e\expnd0\expndtw0 x equivalence. “So, you are _________.”\charscalex100 \par\pard\sl-200\slmult0\fs20 \par\pard\sl-184\slmult0\fs18 \par\pard\li103\sl-280\slmult0\f19\fs28\charscalex99 3.\charscale